#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""The review's feature grid, as a tool: one probe per row, three counts.

    tools/feature-sweep                      run every probe through cpp11
    tools/feature-sweep --clang clang++      and validate each probe with clang first
    tools/feature-sweep --list               print the probes and stop
    CXX1=path/to/cxx3.exe                   the compiler under test (default ./cxx3.exe)

The critical review of 2026-10-08 (`CppOptimize-critical-review_1.md`, section 2)
put 122 probes of C++11 features through cpp11 and found 56 refused, which is
what moved the headline from "C++11" to "C++03 with C++11 extensions". This is
that grid kept as a program, so the headline can be re-measured rather than
re-argued: `docs/REVIEW-PLAN-2026-10-08.md` section 5.2 reads its three counts
at the release step and lets the headline go back only if they say so.

Every probe is a complete program that is valid C++11, or one the standard
refuses whose row says so (`expect="refuse"`: a C++14 form, which cpp11 must
refuse *naming the version*). With `--clang` each probe is first compiled by
clang `-std=c++11 -pedantic-errors -fsyntax-only`, and a probe clang answers
differently from its row is reported as the sweep's own fault, not the
compiler's - the sweep is only an oracle while its programs are. On a
Windows-hosted clang pass `-fno-delayed-template-parsing` through CLANGFLAGS,
or the two-phase-lookup row is accepted by the oracle too; and that clang has
no C++11 library to offer (Microsoft's STL refuses `-std=c++11`, and there is
no Darwin or Linux SDK on the box), so every probe that includes a header is
reported as the oracle's fault there and is validated on the Mac instead -
measured 2026-10-08, and why the fault list is printed rather than gated on.

Each probe lands in one of three bins, and the plan's release step wants the
third at zero:

    compiles              cpp11 accepted it
    refused by name       the message says "not supported yet" or names a standard version
    refused naming nothing  a diagnostic about something else - the invisible bucket
"""
import os
